  "salt": "dZm0urPLRNNFsBrrGUT-Ew==",
  "text": "Morris dancing was a British spring rite. Dancers wore pleated white shirts, white trousers, ribboned caps, and bells. A fiddler played while the troupe stepped and jumped in time. A leader carried a sword with a cake fixed on its point. The dance grew from a rite of spring renewal. The sword carried a phallic echo, and so did the maypole that the dancers often ringed. The feast was Beltane. Beltane opened the summer season. English spring rites gathered around the decorated maypole. The full rite brought in a cut young tree and raised it in the town square. In the Cotswolds the maypole stood with fresh budded boughs and a china doll set among flowers. The Puritan law of England banned the dance in 1644. The rite survives today as a staged folk revival.",
